followed by a term, and "term" includes tag(+KU), which includes "ca", but
I guess my question is "why can GOI be followed by any term, and not solely
by a sumti? What does it even mean, exactly? Exactly for the type of
example you have here? "lo gerku ne ri'u cu blabi" ("The dog, which is on
the right, is white"?) Although that makes things like like "la maik goi
fa'a cu damva'u" parseable but unfathomable (I mean, okay, there at least
an aleph-null amount of utterances that are parseable as well as
unfathomable, but this just seems to be fundamentally weird, although it
does allow tags on non-brivla-based sumti (KOhA, ZO+valsi, LA+cmevla, etc),
which wouldn't otherwise be possible, which I guess directly relates to the
original question.

   Gee, I've come around 180 degrees from my first impression.  I guess it
IS a pretty good thing.  Wish it had been mentioned in the CLL, though,
which I don't see.

